# Methods and Inheritance: OOP
<!-- markdownlint-disable MD024 -->

> **Readings**: Chapters 4 -- 5, chapters 8 -- 11 in the **Pekka's Java Language Manual**.

In this lesson, we will look at how *classes* and *methods* can be used to improve our program code.

There are several important things to understand before proceeding further:

1. *Methods* are blocks of code that **can** (and most likely will be) reused throughout your own and other's Java program code
1. *Methods* must belong to a *class*. For example, `System.out.println()` belongs to `java.lang.System`, not to the `ClassX` class in this lesson
1. All classes that have a main() method automatically have a special method, called *main()*, that takes a string array argument (such as the ones we used in the `Hello` class)
1. All classes that extend another class are automatically "inherit" the methods from its parent class (or parent *superclass*). You can override a method by creating a method with the same signature as the parent method, and providing your own behaviour for that method (instead of what the parent's method did)

First, we will cover *Methods* and their use to improve our code. Then we will see how *Inheritance* is used in our programs.

<ins id="method-overview"></ins>

## Methods in a Nutshell

We have already seen quite a lot of *classes*, but not many of their members (*members* are the things *inside* a class -- in our case, variables and methods). A method belongs to a class.

