You can see a few of the things that make the above solution different than the others. A couple are shown below.

First, we have a local variable to hold the string writer. This is a good idea to keep around to prevent a lot of string copies.

Second, in the production loop, we do not make the call to the environment for production. This is so as not to actually run that part in the debugging code.

Third, we have a special comment in the code saying that the conditional is "always true" when in production.

Finally, there is a little call site comment in the last line near where the call to the debug output function. This allows the assistants to know where the call should be. If they are being careful, the assistants can even figure out where the environment has been stripped out and just assume the environment is production (although this may be less accurate than they can actually be).

