# Java 9 – Optional (Part 1)

[](https://blog.codefx.org/author/timpietrusky/) – January 30, 2018

When Java’s [Guava](https://github.com/google/guava/wiki/UsingOptionalExplained), [`Optional`](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/Optional.html), came out, Java developers got excited on seeing more and more use cases of it, and now Guava’s Optional is available directly in java.util.

Let’s take a look on how to use [Optional](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/Optional.html). Optional is an immutable container which represents a value which can be present or absent. In computer science, absence of a value is equivalent to the same as assigning a null reference in Java, but the absence of a value is still useful. The important point, Optional is a wrapper, which is the reason it’s immutable — if you try to change its value, you end up with a NullPointerException.

## Let’s begin by creating an Optional using Java 8 and Guava

