<|assistant|>
I'll start with the if-statement. It's used to choose different actions, based on some conditions being met - it can be used in a way like "if there are 10 cars, buy more cars". So, the statement itself is an action to use.
A if-statement needs an expression to check. In this text it is an "if there are 10 cars" and it is a condition that returns a boolean value (true or false). This is an important part of all ``if statement``. You can not put a ``void`` as expression. The boolean value of the if-expression will determine what will happen with the rest of the if-statement. In this code the if-expression is something like ``amountOfCars >= 10``. That means that if the cars has a value of 10 or bigger it executes the code inside.
Now this is the ``if`` statement, the next part is what happens when we got the result of the expression. There is the ``If`` condition (or ``Else``). The ``If`` will only be executed, when we get the return value of the if-expression as ``true``. The ``else`` statement is executed, when we got the return value as ``false``.
By the simple condition and the result, we have made what we call a true-false-decision.

