When you send the block to be evaluated from outside (as e.g. at the bottom of a user action), evaluation of that block only happens once the message is evaluated. Evaluating the function has the effect of adding a new message that evaluates the block one more time with a flag that the block was evaluated (for instance to be able to update the interface). So the bottom message never actually gets executed by the server, but it does get sent to be evaluated on the server.

This is why you get the message you get. The block that you return is merely that for the server to execute (evaluate), but nobody needs to actually execute it. The server does the block and sends its return value together with the flag, but in all the cases (that I tried), the return value was not executed by the server.

So the only two possibilities are that

1. it is a server-side component that does not get actually evaluated upon request (in this case, it will be fixed when it is run, since this is where it is called)
2. something else changed since the last time the server executes it, so that the server got the value again, then updates the interface accordingly
3. it is one of the clients who never got executed (you can tell by the way the message is logged. It shows the server has sent a message to the user which is executed by the user, which in this case is not happening since the server hasn't sent the message yet)

To answer your question, the server should have seen the message but doesn't do anything when the block is called. So if you send a new message to an existing user, the message is evaluated when it is sent to the server, which updates the interface, but the server can't execute the body block again (with the right value, since it already evaluated it and put it aside in a global context variable).

