matching the above form, but also be present in the global token dictionary. The exact syntax is `[<user>][<system>]key_<identifier>`, 
though `<user>` or `<system>` will not appear after a key_<identifier> since the key_ identifier will be replaced by the actual key's 
string that you specified.

The key_ identifier works only on Java and C++ applications, but currently the only keys for any other target language can be created 
by calling the `add_key` function in the target app. 

The key_ identifier will be replaced by a string. If you want to provide an actual string you create your own key_<identifier> by 
using a key_ identifier with <identifier> as your value. 

